["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>The calendar tells you where a white tail is and what it wants. October and November change everything.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Year wheel for white tailed deer. Outer ring, antlers: cast January to March, growing in velvet April to August, hard antler September to December. Middle ring: rubs and scrapes build late October, the rut peaks in November, a small second rut in mid December, and fawns are born late May to June. Inner grey ring: winter range from mid December to March.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The white tail year</text><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"164\" r=\"108\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><path d=\"M200.0 56.0 A 108 108 0 0 1 308.0 164.0 L288.0 164.0 A 88 88 0 0 0 200.0 76.0 Z\" fill=\"#c9b79c\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M308.0 164.0 A 108 108 0 0 1 106.5 218.0 L123.8 208.0 A 88 88 0 0 0 288.0 164.0 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M106.5 218.0 A 108 108 0 0 1 200.0 56.0 L200.0 76.0 A 88 88 0 0 0 123.8 208.0 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M118.2 137.4 A 86 86 0 0 1 142.5 100.1 L155.8 115.0 A 66 66 0 0 0 137.2 143.6 Z\" fill=\"#f2b48e\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M125.5 121.0 A 86 86 0 0 1 157.0 89.5 L167.0 106.8 A 66 66 0 0 0 142.8 131.0 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M169.2 83.7 A 86 86 0 0 1 186.5 79.1 L189.7 98.8 A 66 66 0 0 0 176.3 102.4 Z\" fill=\"#f2b48e\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M254.1 230.8 A 86 86 0 0 1 191.0 249.5 L193.1 229.6 A 66 66 0 0 0 241.5 215.3 Z\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M183.4 102.2 A 64 64 0 0 1 200.0 100.0 L200.0 120.0 A 44 44 0 0 0 188.6 121.5 Z\" fill=\"#9e9e9e\" opacity=\"0.7\"/><path d=\"M200.0 100.0 A 64 64 0 0 1 264.0 164.0 L244.0 164.0 A 44 44 0 0 0 200.0 120.0 Z\" fill=\"#9e9e9e\" opacity=\"0.7\"/><text x=\"232.09356159271258\" y=\"49.225197540155534\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Jan</text><text x=\"287.6812408671319\" y=\"81.31875913286811\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Feb</text><text x=\"319.77480245984447\" y=\"136.90643840728742\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mar</text><text x=\"319.77480245984447\" y=\"201.09356159271258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Apr</text><text x=\"287.6812408671319\" y=\"256.6812408671319\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">May</text><text x=\"232.09356159271258\" y=\"288.77480245984447\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Jun</text><text x=\"167.90643840728742\" y=\"288.77480245984447\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Jul</text><text x=\"112.31875913286811\" y=\"256.6812408671319\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Aug</text><text x=\"80.22519754015555\" y=\"201.0935615927126\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sep</text><text x=\"80.22519754015553\" y=\"136.90643840728742\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Oct</text><text x=\"112.31875913286808\" y=\"81.31875913286811\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nov</text><text x=\"167.90643840728745\" y=\"49.225197540155534\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dec</text><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"164\" r=\"42\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rut:</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nov</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Outer: antlers</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b6b6b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Grey: winter range</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Orange: rut</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Blue: fawns born</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Antlers: drop in winter, grow in summer, hard by September <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Rut peaks in November. Fawns: late May or June <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what this chapter covers</summary><ul><li>January to December, one screen per season.</li><li>October and November in detail: rubs, scrapes, rut phases, the doe's heat.</li><li>The antler cycle.</li><li>Winter survival.</li><li>Mule deer migrate much farther. Compare <a href=\"#/s/mc-mule-deer-05\">Mule deer Masterclass 5</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Winter valley cross section with deep snow. Deer bunch up under dense conifers on the left and pack trails between feeding spots. A south facing slope on the right melts out first in the sun. Over about 40 cm of snow, travel gets hard.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deep snow: yard up in conifers</text><path d=\"M0 260 L0 120 L150 230 L250 230 L400 90 L400 260 Z\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#b9c7d6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"260\" width=\"400\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><polygon points=\"40,134.0 27.0,168.0 53.0,168.0\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"37.5\" y=\"168.0\" width=\"5.0\" height=\"8.0\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><polygon points=\"70,156.5 57.0,190.5 83.0,190.5\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"67.5\" y=\"190.5\" width=\"5.0\" height=\"8.0\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><polygon points=\"100,179.0 87.0,213.0 113.0,213.0\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"97.5\" y=\"213.0\" width=\"5.0\" height=\"8.0\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><g transform=\"translate(80 222) scale(0.35 0.35)\"><rect x=\"-41\" y=\"12\" width=\"6\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"-31\" y=\"12\" width=\"6\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"12\" width=\"6\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"37\" y=\"12\" width=\"6\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"52\" ry=\"22\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><path d=\"M-30 -10 L-52 -42 L-40 -48 L-18 -14 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"-58\" cy=\"-46\" rx=\"15\" ry=\"8\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><path d=\"M-50 -52 L-44 -64 L-40 -52 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><circle cx=\"-64\" cy=\"-47\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#23261f\"/><path d=\"M50 -8 C 60 -6 64 6 60 18 C 54 16 50 6 48 -2 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(120 236) scale(-0.3 0.3)\"><rect x=\"-41\" y=\"12\" width=\"6\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"-31\" y=\"12\" width=\"6\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"12\" width=\"6\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><rect x=\"37\" y=\"12\" width=\"6\" height=\"40\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" rx=\"52\" ry=\"22\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><path d=\"M-30 -10 L-52 -42 L-40 -48 L-18 -14 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><ellipse cx=\"-58\" cy=\"-46\" rx=\"15\" ry=\"8\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><path d=\"M-50 -52 L-44 -64 L-40 -52 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/><circle cx=\"-64\" cy=\"-47\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#23261f\"/><path d=\"M50 -8 C 60 -6 64 6 60 18 C 54 16 50 6 48 -2 Z\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></g><path d=\"M60 236 C 100 250 150 252 190 244\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Packed trails</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">South slope:</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">sun melts snow</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Conifer cover</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"294\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Over about 40 cm of snow, travel gets hard.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Valley bottoms, south slopes, dense conifer in deep snow <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Bucks drop antlers January to March <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: winter detail</summary><ul><li>Snow depth is the main factor in where white tails can live (85%, BC account).</li><li>Winter range: south to southwest slopes, fans and terraces with shrubs; cottonwood and willow groves early in winter; conifer patches when snow is deep (85%, BC account, East Kootenay data).</li><li>In winter they are active most of the day, not just dawn and dusk (85%, BC account).</li><li>In hard winters they <strong>yard up</strong> in dense cover and pack trails <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Deer can use snow as water in winter (80%, Montana State University Extension).</li><li>Antlers drop after testosterone falls, about January to March <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Winter browse near Kamloops: Douglas fir peaks in December and January in deer rumens (85%, Willms 1976, mostly mule deer data).</li><li>Leave wintering deer alone: every run costs fat they cannot replace (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-fawn-bedded.jpg\" alt=\"A hidden fawn lies flat and still. Leave it.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A hidden fawn lies flat and still. Leave it. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_deer_fawn_(50131622062).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-saskatoon-flowers.jpg\" alt=\"Saskatoon blooms: green up time.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Saskatoon blooms: green up time. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Robert Flogaus-Faust, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amelanchier_alnifolia_2_RF.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Fields and riverbanks green first <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Fawns born late May or June, hidden about 2 weeks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: spring detail</summary><ul><li>Antlers start growing in April, in velvet <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Pregnant does drive off last year's young before giving birth <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Gestation is about 202 days, roughly 7 months (80%, Montana State University Extension; US Forest Service species review range 187 to 213 days).</li><li>Fawns are born late May and June (85%, BC account; Montana State University Extension). Twins are normal <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Fawns weigh 2 to 4 kg (5 to 8 lb) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Does choose 0.4 to 2 ha (1 to 5 acre) patches with low shrubs and trees to give birth (80%, Montana State University Extension).</li><li>The doe hides the fawn and comes back only to nurse, about 4 to 6 times a day <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Fawns lie still about 95% of the day for the first 10 to 14 days (75%, Virginia DWR, Department of Wildlife Resources).</li><li>Fawns have almost no scent at first, and the doe licks them clean <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Found a lone fawn? It is not abandoned. Leave it (Tip, agency advice).</li><li>The fawn photo above is a mule deer fawn: white tail fawns look the same at this age <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline January to December. Antlers are cast from January to March, grow in velvet from April to August, the velvet is rubbed off in early September, and the antlers stay hard from September to December. Day length drives the cycle through testosterone.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Antler year</text><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"72.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">F</text><text x=\"100.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><text x=\"128.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"156.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><text x=\"184.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"212.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"240.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"268.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">S</text><text x=\"296.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text><text x=\"324.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">N</text><text x=\"352.0\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">D</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cast (drop)</text><rect x=\"30\" y=\"58\" width=\"84\" height=\"22\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#c9b79c\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Grow in velvet</text><rect x=\"114\" y=\"110\" width=\"140\" height=\"22\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"154\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rub velvet off</text><rect x=\"254\" y=\"162\" width=\"17\" height=\"22\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"206\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Hard antler</text><rect x=\"268\" y=\"214\" width=\"98\" height=\"22\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"254\" x2=\"366\" y2=\"254\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"294\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Day length drives it, through testosterone.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Bucks in bachelor groups, antlers in velvet <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Fawns start following the doe at 3 to 4 weeks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: summer detail</summary><ul><li>Summer: floodplains, river terraces, hay and alfalfa fields (85%, BC account; data/migration.json).</li><li>Bucks in velvet avoid fights and stay in cool, secure, insect free spots (80%, Montana State University Extension).</li><li>Bachelor groups of 2 to 4 bucks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Summer activity peaks at dawn and dusk (85%, BC account).</li><li>Fawns lose spots as their first winter coat comes in, in late summer and fall <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-deer-rub.jpg\" alt=\"A buck rub: bark stripped from a small tree at knee to waist height.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A buck rub: bark stripped from a small tree at knee to waist height. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Wasp32, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deer_rub.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Velvet comes off in early to mid September <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Bachelor groups break up. Sparring starts <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: September detail</summary><ul><li>Rising testosterone, driven by shorter days, hardens the antler and the buck rubs the velvet off (75%, University of Georgia).</li><li>First rubs appear on saplings <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Bachelor groups split up through September and October <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Buck season opens. Region 3: bucks 10 September to 10 December <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>. Region 8: bucks 10 September to 30 November <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>. Details in chapter 10.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law. Region 3 page 34, Region 8 page 68, 2026 to 2028 synopsis.</p></aside></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline from 1 October to mid December. Rubs and sparring through October. Scrapes from about mid October to mid November. Seeking from late October into early November. Chasing in early to mid November. Peak breeding in mid November. Post rut and a smaller second rut from late November to mid December. Timing is from hunter guides, not exact dates.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">October to December: the rut builds</text><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#c9b79c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"24.0\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">1 Oct</text><line x1=\"167\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"167\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#c9b79c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"170.8421052631579\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">1 Nov</text><line x1=\"309\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"309\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#c9b79c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"312.94736842105266\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">1 Dec</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"147\" height=\"24\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/><text x=\"26.0\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rubs, sparring</text><rect x=\"105\" y=\"78\" width=\"128\" height=\"24\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><text x=\"111.26315789473684\" y=\"95\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Scrapes</text><rect x=\"124\" y=\"112\" width=\"76\" height=\"24\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#f2b48e\"/><text x=\"206.0\" y=\"129\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Seeking</text><rect x=\"172\" y=\"146\" width=\"66\" height=\"24\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"243.89473684210526\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Chasing</text><rect x=\"219\" y=\"180\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#9b2c1c\"/><text x=\"248.6315789473684\" y=\"197\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Peak breeding</text><rect x=\"257\" y=\"214\" width=\"123\" height=\"24\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#c9b79c\"/><text x=\"262.8421052631579\" y=\"231\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Post rut, 2nd rut</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hunter guide timing, not exact dates. See More.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Rubs early, scrapes late in the month <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Bucks start moving in daylight late October <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: October detail</summary><ul><li>Early October: sparring, rubs, bucks still on summer feeding patterns <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Mid to late October: scrapes appear and get worked at night <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Late October: the <strong>seeking</strong> phase. Bucks cruise for the first does in heat (60%, hunter guides).</li><li>Yearling bucks disperse, mostly October and November (80%, Pennsylvania).</li><li>Antlerless white tails: Region 3 and Region 8, 10 to 31 October, no draw (99%, pages 34 and 68).</li><li>Food shifts: hay regrowth, last green forbs, then browse <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hunter's move: hunt trails between bedding and food, add soft grunts late in the month (Tip, chapter 9).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Calendar bars, October to December. White tailed deer rut peaks in November. Mule deer rut runs mid November to mid December. Opening day pressure pushes deer to thick cover within about 48 hours.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <g fill=\"#dfe6d3\"><rect x=\"100\" y=\"40\" width=\"96\" height=\"190\"/><rect x=\"292\" y=\"40\" width=\"96\" height=\"190\"/></g>\n  <g font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"148\" y=\"32\">Oct</text><text x=\"244\" y=\"32\">Nov</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"32\">Dec</text></g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"80\" font-weight=\"bold\">Whitetail</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"96\">rut peak</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"146\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mule deer</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"162\">rut</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"206\" font-weight=\"bold\">Hunters</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"222\">push</text></g>\n  <rect x=\"196\" y=\"72\" width=\"96\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n  <text x=\"244\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">November</text>\n  <rect x=\"244\" y=\"138\" width=\"96\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n  <text x=\"292\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">mid to mid</text>\n  <polygon points=\"104,214 104,190 120,202\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/>\n  <text x=\"126\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#c0392b\">opener: thick cover, night,</text>\n  <text x=\"126\" y=\"224\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#c0392b\">within about 48 h (65%)</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Rut timing 80%. In the rut, bucks move</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">more and in daylight (75%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Peak breeding mid November <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Old bucks move midday. Be out all day (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: November detail</summary><ul><li>The BC account puts the white tail rut peak in November <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Penn State: most does conceive about 13 to 17 November <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. The Thompson peak is not measured: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> local dates.</li><li>Northern white tails breed in a tight window of about a month (85%, US Forest Service species review).</li><li><strong>Chasing</strong> in early to mid November, then <strong>tending</strong> (lockdown) when bucks stay with does (60%, hunter guides).</li><li>Bucks barely eat and lose weight fast <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Rattling and grunting work best now <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Region 3: bucks open to 10 December. Region 8: bucks close 30 November. Youth only either sex 1 to 30 November in both (99%, pages 34 and 68).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline of one doe. A buck tends her for 1 to 3 days before her heat, she is in heat for about 24 hours, and he stays 2 to 3 days after. If she is not bred, she comes into heat again about 28 days later, which is why a second rut shows in December.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">One doe, one heat</text><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"60\" y=\"120\" width=\"90\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#f2b48e\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Buck tends</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1 to 3 days</text><rect x=\"150\" y=\"100\" width=\"34\" height=\"50\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"167\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Heat 24 h</text><rect x=\"184\" y=\"120\" width=\"70\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#f2b48e\"/><text x=\"219\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Tends</text><text x=\"226\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2 to 3 days</text><path d=\"M184 196 C 230 250 320 250 350 196\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 5\"/><line x1=\"345\" y1=\"205\" x2=\"352\" y2=\"194\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"352.0,194.0 351.0,202.9 344.3,198.7\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Not bred? Heat again in about 28 days</text><rect x=\"340\" y=\"110\" width=\"30\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".6\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"294\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">This is why a 2nd rut shows in December.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>In heat about 24 hours <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Not bred? Heat again about 28 days later <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: estrus detail</summary><ul><li>A doe is in heat (estrus) about 24 hours (80%, Montana State University Extension; US Forest Service species review says 24 to 48 hours).</li><li>A buck <strong>tends</strong> her for 1 to 3 days before and 2 or 3 days after <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Not bred: she comes back into heat at about 28 day intervals (80%; US Forest Service species review gives 21 to 30 days).</li><li>So a small <strong>second rut</strong> shows in mid December (Tip, from the 28 day cycle).</li><li>Doe fawns on good feed can breed at 6 to 7 months, often later than adults (80%, Montana State University Extension).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The deer rut in 4 phases. Pre rut, mid October to early November: bucks rub and check does at dawn and dusk. Seeking and chasing, first half of November: bucks cruise funnels in daylight. Peak, mid November: a buck tends one doe, hunt the does. Post rut, December: bucks thin and back in cover, a small second rut about 28 days later.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"320\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer rut, 4 phases</text>\n  <path d=\"M20 200 Q90 190 130 150 Q190 70 230 60 Q270 70 300 150 Q330 200 380 205\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"5\"/>\n  <path d=\"M330 200 Q345 175 360 200\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">buck activity</text>\n  <path d=\"M20 215 H380\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"55\" y=\"234\">mid Oct</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"234\">early Nov</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"234\">mid Nov</text><text x=\"345\" y=\"234\">Dec</text></g>\n  <g fill=\"#2f3a2b\"><rect x=\"12\" y=\"246\" width=\"88\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/><rect x=\"104\" y=\"246\" width=\"96\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/><rect x=\"204\" y=\"246\" width=\"84\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/><rect x=\"292\" y=\"246\" width=\"96\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\"/></g>\n  <g font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"56\" y=\"268\">Pre rut</text><text x=\"152\" y=\"268\">Seeking</text><text x=\"246\" y=\"268\">Peak</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"268\">Post rut</text></g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#f3d36b\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"56\" y=\"292\">rubs</text><text x=\"152\" y=\"292\">cruising</text><text x=\"246\" y=\"292\">hunt does</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"292\">in cover</text></g>\n  <text x=\"345\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2nd rut</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Bucks worn out, feeding hard, back in cover <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Snow pushes deer to winter range <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: December detail</summary><ul><li>Post rut bucks rest, feed and stay tight to cover <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Early winter: river groves first, then south slopes and conifer patches as snow deepens (65%, data/migration.json).</li><li>Region 3: bucks to 10 December, then bow only either sex 11 to 20 December (99%, page 34).</li><li>Region 8: bow only either sex 1 to 20 December (99%, page 68).</li><li>Late season deer are lean. Food is the key: find the best remaining browse (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Year wheel for white tailed deer. Outer ring, antlers: cast January to March, growing in velvet April to August, hard antler September to December. Middle ring: rubs and scrapes build late October, the rut peaks in November, a small second rut in mid December, and fawns are born late May to June. Inner grey ring: winter range from mid December to March.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The white tail year</text><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"164\" r=\"108\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/><path d=\"M200.0 56.0 A 108 108 0 0 1 308.0 164.0 L288.0 164.0 A 88 88 0 0 0 200.0 76.0 Z\" fill=\"#c9b79c\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M308.0 164.0 A 108 108 0 0 1 106.5 218.0 L123.8 208.0 A 88 88 0 0 0 288.0 164.0 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M106.5 218.0 A 108 108 0 0 1 200.0 56.0 L200.0 76.0 A 88 88 0 0 0 123.8 208.0 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M118.2 137.4 A 86 86 0 0 1 142.5 100.1 L155.8 115.0 A 66 66 0 0 0 137.2 143.6 Z\" fill=\"#f2b48e\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M125.5 121.0 A 86 86 0 0 1 157.0 89.5 L167.0 106.8 A 66 66 0 0 0 142.8 131.0 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M169.2 83.7 A 86 86 0 0 1 186.5 79.1 L189.7 98.8 A 66 66 0 0 0 176.3 102.4 Z\" fill=\"#f2b48e\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M254.1 230.8 A 86 86 0 0 1 191.0 249.5 L193.1 229.6 A 66 66 0 0 0 241.5 215.3 Z\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" opacity=\"1\"/><path d=\"M183.4 102.2 A 64 64 0 0 1 200.0 100.0 L200.0 120.0 A 44 44 0 0 0 188.6 121.5 Z\" fill=\"#9e9e9e\" opacity=\"0.7\"/><path d=\"M200.0 100.0 A 64 64 0 0 1 264.0 164.0 L244.0 164.0 A 44 44 0 0 0 200.0 120.0 Z\" fill=\"#9e9e9e\" opacity=\"0.7\"/><text x=\"232.09356159271258\" y=\"49.225197540155534\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Jan</text><text x=\"287.6812408671319\" y=\"81.31875913286811\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Feb</text><text x=\"319.77480245984447\" y=\"136.90643840728742\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Mar</text><text x=\"319.77480245984447\" y=\"201.09356159271258\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Apr</text><text x=\"287.6812408671319\" y=\"256.6812408671319\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">May</text><text x=\"232.09356159271258\" y=\"288.77480245984447\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Jun</text><text x=\"167.90643840728742\" y=\"288.77480245984447\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Jul</text><text x=\"112.31875913286811\" y=\"256.6812408671319\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Aug</text><text x=\"80.22519754015555\" y=\"201.0935615927126\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sep</text><text x=\"80.22519754015553\" y=\"136.90643840728742\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Oct</text><text x=\"112.31875913286808\" y=\"81.31875913286811\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nov</text><text x=\"167.90643840728745\" y=\"49.225197540155534\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dec</text><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"164\" r=\"42\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rut:</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nov</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Outer: antlers</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b6b6b\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Grey: winter range</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Orange: rut</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Blue: fawns born</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Spring in velvet, fall in bone; November bucks won't leave does alone.\"</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 202 days, 24 hours, 28 days.</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule</strong> (tradition): \"When the leaves are down and the frost is hard, the old bucks walk in daylight.\"</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks, mistakes and field drill</summary><ul><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 13 to 17 November: most does bred (Penn State). Count back 202 days: early June fawns.</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule</strong> (opinion, widely repeated): \"Hunt rubs in October, scrapes before peak, does in the rut.\"</li><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Antlers drop when days grow long; velvet's off when the rut's song's strong.\"</li></ul><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Hunting only weekends in November and missing the midweek peak.</li><li>Expecting a rut on a fixed date every year.</li><li>Pushing wintering deer for photos or shed antlers.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>From 15 October, log every rub, scrape and chasing buck you see, with date and place. In 3 years you will know your valley's rut.</p></aside></details>","<ul><li>BC Ministry of Environment, <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/whttail.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">White tailed deer in BC</a> (Blood 2000), via <code>research/2026-10-03-migration-and-areas.md</code> (85%, 70% for the Thompson)</li></ul><ul><li>Knight, Montana State University Extension, <a href=\"https://animalrangeextension.montana.edu/wildlife/documents/whitetail-mgmt.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">White tailed deer management for Montana landowners</a>, read 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></li></ul><ul><li>USDA Forest Service US Forest Service species review, <a href=\"https://www.fs.usda.gov/database/feis/animals/mammal/odvi/all.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">white tailed deer review</a>, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li></ul><ul><li>University of Georgia, <a href=\"https://fieldreport.caes.uga.edu/news/deer-antlers-grow-and-shed-as-summer-days-shorten/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">antlers grow and shed as days shorten</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></li></ul><ul><li>Virginia DWR, <a href=\"https://dwr.virginia.gov/blog/the-most-important-week-of-a-fawns-life\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">most important week of a fawn's life</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></li></ul><ul><li>Penn State Extension breeding dates, via <code>research/2026-10-01-deer.md</code> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></li></ul>","<ul><li>Guidefitter, <a href=\"https://guidefitter.com/blog/deer-rut-timeline-the-7-stages-and-how-to-hunt-them\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">deer rut timeline</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span></li></ul><ul><li>Willms 1976, <a href=\"https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/pdf/10.4141/cjas76-065\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kamloops deer diets</a>, via <code>research/2026-10-01-deer.md</code> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li></ul><ul><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028</a>, Region 3 page 34, Region 8 page 68, read directly 2026-10-02 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></li></ul><ul><li>Research log: <code>research/2026-10-11-masterclass-white-tailed-deer.md</code>.</li></ul>"]